### Phase L — the admin spine (S48–S50). 12-admin-console.md §3, §5.
###
### Everything here is on :3100. Nothing here answers on :3000, ever — that is
### the point of the phase, and block 0 proves it before anything else runs.
###
### Two things you need before block 2:
###   1. an authenticator app on your phone (any TOTP app), and
###   2. `ADMIN_TOTP_ENC_KEY` in backend/.env:
###        openssl rand -base64 32
###      The admin process refuses to boot without it. That refusal is
###      deliberate — see the docblock in src/admin-main.ts.
###
### Then, in two terminals:
###   cd backend && PORT=3999 npm run dev          # the public API
###   cd backend && npm run dev:admin              # the admin API, :3100

@public = http://localhost:3999
@admin  = http://localhost:3100
@api    = {{admin}}/admin/api/v1

### ─── 0. Isolation. Run these FIRST, and read the status codes. ──────────────

### ★★ 404. Not 401 — the route is not there, and a 401 would confirm it exists.
GET {{public}}/admin/api/v1/users
Accept: application/json

###
### 404 as well. S15's two routes moved here at S49 (12 §11.1).
GET {{public}}/admin/api/v1/metrics

###
### 200 — the admin process is up. The only unauthenticated admin route.
GET {{admin}}/health

###
### 401 — the same path on :3100 exists and refuses you. That contrast IS the
### isolation: 404 there, 401 here.
GET {{api}}/users

### ─── 1. First login: enrollment is mandatory ────────────────────────────────

### ★ Step one. Returns a challenge and NO cookie: a correct password is half
### of an admin login, not all of it.
# @name login
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@local.dev",
  "password": "change-me-before-prod"
}

### → { challengeId, ttlSec: 120, enrollmentRequired: true }
###   `enrollmentRequired` is the seed's `totpEnrolledAt: null` (12 §4.1).
###   The seed never writes a TOTP secret — every deployment of this codebase
###   shipping with the same second factor would be worse than having none.

###
### ★★ Enrollment. The ONLY route an unenrolled admin can reach.
###
### Everything in the response is unrecoverable afterwards: the secret is stored
### only as AES-256-GCM ciphertext and the recovery codes only as sha256 hashes.
### There is deliberately no "show me again" — a route that could show them to
### you could show them to whoever has your cookie.
###
### ★ SAVE THE RECOVERY CODES SOMEWHERE THAT IS NOT THIS MACHINE.
# @name enroll
POST {{api}}/auth/totp/enroll
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "challengeId": "{{login.response.body.challengeId}}"
}

### → { otpauthUri, secret, recoveryCodes: [10], challengeId }
###   Paste `otpauthUri` into your authenticator app (or turn it into a QR with
###   `qrencode -t ansiutf8 '<uri>'`). The app starts showing six digits.

###
### ★ Step two. Replace 000000 with what your phone is showing RIGHT NOW.
POST {{api}}/auth/mfa
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "challengeId": "{{enroll.response.body.challengeId}}",
  "code": "000000"
}

### → 200 + the admin identity, and two cookies:
###     admin_access   15 min, JWT, names the AdminSession
###     admin_refresh  8 h absolute, opaque, HMAC'd into AdminSession.tokenHash
###   Both are Path=/admin, SameSite=Strict. Check them in DevTools.

###
### ★★ NOW RUN THE MFA BLOCK ABOVE AGAIN, UNCHANGED, WITH THE SAME SIX DIGITS.
###
### → 401 INVALID_MFA_CODE, even though the code is still on your phone's
###   screen and still arithmetically correct.
###
###   That is `AdminCredential.lastTotpStep`, and it is the entire value of a
###   second factor: without it, a code is valid for its whole 30-second window
###   however many times it is presented — so shoulder-surfing one, or pulling
###   one out of a proxy log, buys a login.

### ─── 2. The session ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

GET {{api}}/auth/me

### → { id, email, role, mfaAt, stepUpValidUntil, sessionExpiresAt }
###   `stepUpValidUntil` is mfaAt + ADMIN_STEPUP_WINDOW_MIN. The console counts
###   down from it so it can ask for a code BEFORE a destructive action fails.

###
### Rotates admin_refresh. Note what does NOT move: sessionExpiresAt. Rotation
### renews the token, never the 8-hour absolute cap — otherwise a tab left open
### would be a session that never ends.
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh

### ─── 3. The read endpoints (S50) ────────────────────────────────────────────

GET {{api}}/users?limit=10

###
### ★ Search by email, id, or display name. Note: display-name matching is
### case-SENSITIVE on Postgres and not on SQLite — see `UserSearchFilter.query`.
GET {{api}}/users?q=me@test.dev

###
### Replace with a real id from the list above.
GET {{api}}/users/REPLACE_WITH_A_USER_ID

### → the profile, the status history, and the CACHED wallet balances.
###   ★ Deliberately cached, not recomputed: `POST /wallets/:id/reconcile` (M2)
###     is where `Σ ledger` is compared against the column. A detail page that
###     silently reconciled would hide exactly the drift E1 exists to surface.
###   ★ And grep the response for `passwordHash`. It is not there, and a test
###     asserts that it never will be.

###
GET {{api}}/audit?limit=5

### → actor, ip, userAgent, requestId, action, target, reason, before/after,
###   prevHash, hash. The hashes travel so you can recompute a row yourself.

### ─── 4. ★★ The one write, and the three refusals in front of it ─────────────

### Put a real, NON-ADMIN user id here. Do not use your own.
@target = REPLACE_WITH_A_USER_ID

### ★ Refusal 1 — no reason. 400 REASON_REQUIRED, and NOTHING is written.
### `reason` is a column and an error, not a UI placeholder: six months from now
### "who disabled this account" is answerable from the row, and "why" only if
### somebody was made to type it.
POST {{api}}/users/{{target}}/disable
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###
### ★ Refusal 2 — self-target. 409 SELF_TARGET_FORBIDDEN.
### The realistic version of this is not malice: it is an operator with two tabs
### open. Recovery from a console nobody can sign into is CLI plus database
### access, at whatever hour it happens.
POST {{api}}/users/REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_OWN_ADMIN_ID/disable
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "testing the self-target guard" }

###
### ★★ Refusal 3 — the step-up window. To see it, WAIT SIX MINUTES after your
### last code (ADMIN_STEPUP_WINDOW_MIN defaults to 5) and then send the real
### disable below.
###
### → 401 STEP_UP_REQUIRED, and — the half that matters — no audit row and no
###   status change. A step-up checked inside the handler, after the first
###   await, would be a confirmation dialog rather than a control.
###
### Then get past it with a fresh code:
POST {{api}}/auth/stepup
Content-Type: application/json

{ "code": "000000" }

###
### ★ The real thing.
# @name disable
POST {{api}}/users/{{target}}/disable
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reason": "testing the audit spine" }

### → { user: { status: 'DISABLED', statusReason }, refreshFamiliesRevoked,
###     adminSessionsRevoked, auditLogId, controlCommandId }
###
### ★★ THE ONE THING TO LOOK HARDEST AT: open `npm run db:studio` and check the
###    disabled player's Wallet and WalletTransaction rows. The balance is
###    UNCHANGED and there is no new row of any kind.
###
###    That is invariant A8. An ejection for idling forfeits the match (10 §5.1)
###    because forfeiture punishes idling; a platform-initiated interruption is
###    not idling. Without A8, an operator looking into a report would fine the
###    person they were looking into.
###
### And `refreshFamiliesRevoked` should be the number of devices they were
### signed in on — every family, not just the newest. A laptop left signed in
### would otherwise keep minting access tokens for thirty days.

### ─── 5. ★★ The audit chain, and breaking it on purpose ──────────────────────

GET {{api}}/audit?limit=1

### → the row your disable just wrote. Note `prevHash` and `hash`.

###
### Clean, and it says how many rows it walked.
GET {{api}}/audit/verify

### → { ok: true, checked: N, brokenAt: null }

###
### ★★ NOW BREAK IT. In another terminal:
###
###   sqlite3 prisma/dev.db "DELETE FROM AdminAuditLog \
###     WHERE id = (SELECT id FROM AdminAuditLog ORDER BY createdAt LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1)"
###
### then run this again:
GET {{api}}/audit/verify

### → { ok: false, brokenAt: { index: 1, entryId, reason: 'PREV_HASH_MISMATCH',
###     expected, actual } }
###
### ★ `PREV_HASH_MISMATCH` means a row is MISSING from between two survivors —
###   the classic "delete the evidence". Edit a row's `reason` in place instead
###   and you get `HASH_MISMATCH`, because its own content no longer hashes to
###   its own hash. The two are distinguished on purpose: an investigator needs
###   to know whether history was deleted or rewritten.
###
### What this does NOT claim: the chain cannot stop somebody with database
### access from deleting a row. It makes doing so impossible to do QUIETLY —
### every hash after the break has to be rewritten too — which is the strongest
### honest guarantee a log the application writes can offer.

### ─── 6. Lockout ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Log in fresh, then send /auth/mfa with 000000 five times. On the fifth:
### → 423 ADMIN_LOCKED, with `lockedUntil` in the details.
###
### Then check SecurityEvent in Studio: an ALERT row, reason ADMIN_MFA_LOCKOUT.
### Five wrong codes against a console account is somebody working through a
### six-digit space, not an operator fumbling.
###
### ★ And note that the lock holds against the CORRECT code too. A lockout a
###   valid code could lift would only ever inconvenience the legitimate
###   operator, since the attacker does not have one.
